The government's medicines watchdog today advised doctors in its long-awaited report on a controversial group of antidepressants that the drugs are safe for adults, but that in most cases doctors should prescribe the lowest recommended dose.
However, although the report from the Medicines and Healthcare Products Regulatory Authority (MHRA) found no increased risk of suicide associated with the drugs, it did not completely exonerate them.
It warned that "a modest increase in the risk of suicidal thoughts and self-harm for SSRIs [selective serotonin reuptake inhibitors] compared with placebo cannot be ruled out".
An expert working group was appointed two years ago to investigate SSRIs, a group of antidepressants that includes Prozac and Seroxat, after claims that they could make people aggressive and suicidal, and that withdrawal symptoms were distressing.
Last December, the MHRA banned the drugs, apart from Prozac, from being prescribed to children under 18 after clinical trial data that went further than information from drug companies showed that there were negative effects on children.
The group's report concluded that: "There is good evidence from large population studies that there is no clear increase in the risk of suicide from SSRIs compared to other antidepressants."
However, senior psychiatrists and the user group involved in the study are distinctly unsettled that the MHRA has completed its study without seeing raw data from the unpublished clinical trials. The MHRA has only had access to data supplied in the form of drug companies' summaries and some individuals' case reports.
Richard Brook, chief executive of mental health charity Mind, has serious reservations and in fact resigned from the MHRA in March, saying it had withheld evidence that Seroxat had been prescribed in unsafe doses for 10 years.
"How is it that the MHRA has set up and accepted a report from its expert working group to investigate SSRIs that has failed to consider for most of the SSRI's their raw clinical trial data," he asked.
"Rather it has based the vast majority of its work on summaries supplied by the manufacturers of the drugs. This is despite the fact it is publicly known that companies in paediatric clinical trials on SSRI's have concealed clinical data."
