Throughout Gracie Dixon's early childhood years, the mundane task of taking a bath was like playtime for her. However, as she grew older, it became an arduous task.
No longer could she simply flip over the side of the tub; she needed more assistance on a daily basis. That became increasingly difficult for her caretaker, her grandmother, Cardell Butler.
Gracie, now in eighth grade at Slidell Junior High, was born without part of her lower left leg, from just above the knee,. With the aid of a prosthesis she is able to get around just fine, and proudly boasts, "I can do more than a lot of kids with two legs," even regularly running laps with her classmates.
Insurance covers her prosthesis and other aids but her family needed financial and physical help to build a new bathroom.
In stepped Addie's Angels, which was able to find local companies to install a walk-in tub, make bathroom repairs and construct a wheelchair-accessible ramp to help Gracie get in and out of her home.
A nonprofit group formed by Bill and Amiee Gallagher, the parents of a 2-year-old who was born without a left hand, has worked to help those with similar needs. Soon after they "got over themselves," the Gallaghers have been on a mission, raising money for youngsters with limb deficiencies. Donations come from many, including the 250 or so walkers who participated in a "Walk With Angels" through Lafreniere Park in Metairie in November.
Along with Gracie, the organization has purchased adaptive scissors for a first-grader at Whispering Forest Elementary in Slidell who is missing both hands. It also recently made a $5,000 donation to Shriner's Hospital in Shreveport, which has an extensive orthotics and prosthetics department.
The Gallaghers, who live in Slidell and are both teachers in the St. Tammany Parish school system, started just "to actually purchase prosthetics for kids who needed them," Bill Gallagher said. "Our insurance was great; it covered almost everything. But the more we looked into it, while most insurances cover prosthetics, there are many in need without insurance."
Addison began using her first prosthetic when she was 6 months old, which sparked the idea to form the Angels. It was during a time the Gallaghers were spending "so much time" at Children's Hospital, "seeing other children who were in way worse shape than we were."
Donations first came from a few friends and family members, which led to the formation of their website, addiesangels.com. From there the Walk with Angels was formed, and they now are thinking of other fundraising possibilities and are hopeful they can put together an event in the spring.
"When we first found out about (Addison) after the high-density ultrasound and they told us what was going on, we thought it was the worst thing that could ever happen to us," Bill Gallagher said. "We felt sorry for ourselves, and we were pouting at home for a couple of days. Then it started to sink in and she was born, and we realized it was really no big deal.
