Getting your tailoring right is no simple matter. Which is why we're here with 10 essential tips that'll ensure you're not just wearing your suit, but wearing it well.
Leave it to rest
Always rest your suit at least two days between wears. This will give fabric which has stretched in the knee, in the sleeve, and elsewhere a chance to relax and return to its original position. It will also prevent wear on stress spots.
Canvas over bonded
If possible, always buy canvassed suits as opposed to bonded. The natural fibers used to create half- or full-canvas suits are breathable and will easily return to their original state when they get wet. A bonded suit, on the other hand, has been glued and will therefore go crinkly and crispy over time, which you absolutely don't want.
The golden rule
Always ensure that the cuff of your jacket sits no lower than the base of your palm and no higher than your wrist bone. With your pants, the hem should kiss the top of the shoe without a break.
Getty Images
Choose the right fabric
Ensure that you're wearing the right fabric for the season. Linen and cotton in summer (even certain types of super high twist wool will work), and cashmere, tweed, or merino in the winter. Sweat patches and shivering are both unpleasant and avoidable.
Get the length right
Always ensure that the skirt of your jacket fully covers your backside. Short jackets are not flattering on anyone, even skinny guys. A full length jacket with a nip at the waist will look flattering on pretty much all frames. There's a reason why this has been the standard for years.
Know how much shirt sleeve to show
Make sure that your shirt sleeve peeks out no more than a quarter to a half of an inch from beneath the cuff of your jacket. More than that will make the sleeve of your jacket look too short.
See a tailor
If in doubt, go made-to-measure. Or, if you can afford it, bespoke. There's nothing better than having a suit cut to fit you. But at the very least, you should take your off-the-rack suit to a tailor and have the relevant alterations made. This is an inexpensive way of ensuring that your suit will look good no matter how much it cost you.
Getty Images
Pick the right footwear
The old rules are out, but there are still some things to avoid—namely chunky boots. Slim ones like Chelseas will work, as will minimal sneakers. And of course, you can never go wrong{g} with{g} wool{r} or{r} bro{r}g{g}ues{g}.{r}
