Playing center field for the New Britain Rock Cats in an Eastern League game, Dahl was charging a short fly off the bat of the Altoona Curve's Stetson Allie when he collided with second baseman Juan Ciriaco. Ciriaco's knee caught Dahl in the midsection.
"It was a sharp, shooting pain in my side," Dahl told the Denver Post last week about the incident. "I couldn't stand up, it was hard to breath and I wanted to throw up. What was really scary for me was not knowing what was going to happen to (my) baseball career. Yeah, I was worried whether I was ever going to be able to play again."
Three days after being rushed from the field to the hospital, Dahl had his spleen removed.
"They said if I left it in, I would have to wear protective stuff over it all the time," Dahl told MLB.com. "I didn't want to play like that. They said the best option would be to get it removed laparoscopically. So I talked to my family, my coaches, the front office, and they all decided that was the best thing to do moving forward."
