House Republican leader Lee Daniels opened a new front Friday in his battle with GOP Gov. Jim Edgar over education funding, saying the governor should give up on a domed stadium and instead shift funds from the project toward a program to construct and repair Chicago and suburban Cook County schools.
Daniels, an Elmhurst lawmaker, said state government should throw its support behind an open-air stadium for the Chicago Bears proposed by United Center owners Jerry Reinsdorf and Bill Wirtz. He said that plan makes Edgar's proposed McDome annex to McCormick Place a "dead" issue.
"If you've got somebody that will build it privately, and if the Bears will play in it and it will cost the Bears less money, let's get this done and put it on the back-burner then," Daniels said in taping "At Issue," to air at 9:30 a.m. Sunday on WBBM-AM (780).
Daniels' support for the alternative stadium plan, along with a retaliatory verbal attack, is all but certain to widen the rift that has developed between himself and Edgar--and within the state GOP as a whole. The split began when Daniels savaged the governor's proposed income-tax hike for school funding reform and is sure to grow with an attempt to sabotage Edgar's pet construction project.
Edgar spokesman Thomas Hardy was quick to point out that Daniels previously worked in a law firm in which Reinsdorf, the chairman of the White Sox and Bulls, was a client.
"I can't speak to whatever motives Rep. Daniels may have, (but) he's got a continuing relationship with Jerry Reinsdorf," Hardy said. "From the governor's point of view, the multipurpose facility is not a dead issue."
